# Copy to .env.local and fill in from the Supabase dashboard (Settings -> API).
# Both values are public: RLS is what protects the data, not these.
# NEVER put the service role / secret key in this app.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Where the website is. Premium checkout opens `<this>/checkout` in the browser:
# Supabase will not serve HTML from an edge function on its own domain. From an
# Android emulator, localhost reaches the dev server via `adb reverse tcp:3000`.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# Which way this build is distributed, and therefore who takes the money.
#
# `direct` (or unset) -- the APK handed out from eraya.app. It is sideloaded
# rather than distributed by Play, so Play's payments policy does not reach it,
# and it keeps the Razorpay browser checkout it has always had.
#
# `play` -- the build uploaded to Google Play. Play requires digital goods to be
# sold through Google Play Billing, which is not finished, so this build sells
# nothing at all and says so. It must never fall back to Razorpay.
#
# Anything else is read as `unknown` and also sells nothing: a value somebody
# meant to set and mistyped should not be guessed at. Leaving it unset is the
# one case that is not a mistake -- the local Gradle release build of the public
# APK declares nothing, and absent must keep meaning the behaviour already live.
#
# Leave it unset locally. The EAS profiles declare it: `play` in eas.json sets
# `play`, and every other profile sets `direct`. iOS ignores it entirely --
# Premium is not for sale there at all. `npm run play:channel` prints what each
# profile resolves to. See docs/10-payments.md.
EXPO_PUBLIC_DISTRIBUTION=
